On Tue, Jan 08, 2002 at 12:26:51PM -0500, Kurt A. Ludwig wrote:
>
> IIRC PC164 should be ev56. PC164 PC164LX and PC164UX should be, whereas
> PC164SX is pca56. IIRC PC164RX is pca57, but don't quote me on that =)

OK, quote *me*, then... :-)

EB164 family (HWRPB sys_type field == EB164)

variation
(HWRPB
sys_variation	CPU name	core logic	product name or
field)		(MHz range)	chipset		alias
-------------	-------------	----------	-------
EB164		EV5 (233-333)	CIA-1		EB164
PC164		EV56 (433-500)	CIA-2		AlphaPC164
LX164		EV56 (466-600)	PYXIS		AlphaPC164-LX
SX164		PCA56 (533)	PYXIS		AlphaPC164-SX
RX164		PCA57 (525)	POLARIS		AlphaPC164-RX (no product)

EB164 was the first Evaluation Board product from the old Digital Semi
group to employ EV5, which was (at least) twice as fast as the EV45
@275MHz found in the previous generation's AlphaPC64 (Cabriolet).

PC164 introduced EV56 with its byte/word instructions, but paired it with
the CIA-2 core logic which forced the use of SPARSE I/O space addressing
for byte/word quantities.

LX164 introduced PYXIS into the EB-series (MIATA aka PWS was first to
productize PYXIS), with its ability to do I/O space byte/word access.

SX164 introduced PCA56, which adds the so-called MVI instructions to
those supported by EV56, but doesn't perform as well as EV56.

And the (non-productized) RX164 (would have) introduced PCA57, AFAICT
just a respin/optimize of PCA56, which performed nearly as well as
EV56. Oh, and also POLARIS core logic, which was 32-bit PCI only.

Somebody file this in the FAQ... ;-}

--Jay++

