Well, I just found an elderly linux-kernel posting which says that the newer
mke2fs use sparse superblocks, which cannot be read by MILO. So the
partition where the kernel called from MILO resides has to be formatted with
"mke2fs /dev/xxx -O none" (see the mke2fs man page for details).
I also use an old (2.0.35) MILO to boot into RH7.1 (the RH7.1 MILO didnt
work), and the above trick did the job.

hope that helps
	Oliver


