t wrote:

> ...of converting to SRM but couldn't get a good boot and had to go back to
> MILO.

If it works, don't fix it.  I converted to SRM on four PC164s
and had problems with unsupported SCSI cards and
older ide drives.  Milo supports just about anything at boot
time.  If you like to learn new (albeit fading into obsolescence)
things and tinker, then install SRM and buy a Maxtor IDE drive
for booting (and perhaps for general use).  That's what I did,
but I like to waste my time tinkering.
If you are using old SCSI hardware, you may
even find that the new DMA100 IDE drives actually perform
better from the hdparm view.

>  And I haven't used my Alpha since then, but I want to get it going
> again with the 2.4 kernel.

2.4 has not been stable on my PC164s, but then I haven't
tried the latest versions and I haven't followed the threads
about fixing 2.4 for axp.  2.2.17 does a fine job if you just
want to get up and running.

> If there is some more recent SRM code I would love to give it a try
> (especially with RH 7.2 soon to arrive).

Apparently development on SRM for the PC164
stopped some years back.

Bob







