I ran into a strange problem between Netscape 4.77 and XFree
4.1.0 on a Redhat 7.1 system (on a UP2000).  The first time I
invoke netscape under a given X server, the mouse cursor turns
into an unfamiliar square box with a symmetrical pattern in
it.  Clicking with the left mouse button restores the cursor
to a normal appearance, but if there's a window under the
cursor at the time of the click, the window dies with the
following message returned to the shell from which it was
invoked:

    X connection to :2.0 broken (explicit kill or server
    shutdown).

Is this anything anyone else has ever seen?

After running Netscape once, I can kill it, restart it, etc.
without anything unusual.

The hardware is a UP2000, one processor, TNT2 Vanta graphics
card, plus a bunch of other PCI cards.

The system software is RH7.1 plus up2date updates as of Jan.
12.  I built XFree86 4.1.0 from the source tar files from
www.xfree86.org (IIRC) and installed in /usr/local/X11R6.
I added /usr/local/X11R6/lib to /etc/ld.so.conf to catch the
.so libraries.  Other than having to fiddle with with the
erase character (Ctrl-H vs. DEL), it seems to work a lot more
cleanly than did 4.0.3, at least with 16-bit color.

It's puzzling that it only happens the first time Netscape is
invoked, and Netscape is apparently the only thing program
that causes this kind of behavior.

Any suggestions?

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




