Rob Byrnes wrote:

> I think the Debian install is fine.  Debian 2.2r3 was my first Linux 
> distro, and is still the only linux distro I use.  Since Alpha boxen 
> have limited options in terms of expansion cards etc. I found the 
> install is simpler than on i386.  apt is possibly one of the most 
> daunting, yet powerful command-line 

Daunting?  I've found dselect with apt to be powerful and easy to use 
from the very beginning.  It is not easy to install, but it is 
straightforward: if you know where you want partitions (or if you 
already have partitions), and if you know what modules to install (a big 
stumbling block for newbies!), then you're all set.  And once it's 
installed, Debian is an absolute dream to maintain; although others have 
caught up to much of the functionality of apt, debconf ("install wizards 
done right") and other tools (update-alternatives, etc.) have yet to be 
duplicated anywhere, free or non-free.

Other reasons I'm a Debian chauvanist can be found at 
http://lyre.mit.edu/~powell/debian.html , but for the purposes of the 
present discussion, the most important is that no corporate cost-cutting 
or even bankruptcy will stop a Debian release from happening.  While 
others have been retreating from non-i386 arches, Debian keeps adding 
more: six arches shipped with ~4000 packages in potato (i386, alpha, 
PPC, Sparc, ARM, m68k), and there are ELEVEN with at least seven 
thousand packages in woody (same six plus IA-64, S/390, MIPS, MIPSel, HPPA)!

