With regards to your message at 02:54 PM 1/15/02, Mike Foley. Where you stated:
>Maurice,
>
>
>         That's a cheap shot. There's alot more to the UP1500
>         story that's not fit for this forum. Suffice to say, weight
>         was thrown around on all sides. Remember, API was a Samsung
>         company, not the other way around.

Actually API was a Samsung and Compaq company.

For most of a year, after Samsung announced the UP1500, we would ask API if 
they were going to be distributing it, and they said "maybe".
When the time came 10 months ago, where Samsung started booking orders for 
these, we again asked API and got no firm answer.
We then asked Samsung if they would mind selling to us, and they checked 
with API, who did everything they could to stop this.
In the meantime Samsung people made arrangements so that we could book 
them, but not in North America.
Finally API folded it's tent, and those silly days are over.

Cheap shot? No I don't think so. I think API did as much as possible to 
discourage Alpha development as they could. Perhaps not intentionally, but 
that was the net effect.


>  > API did not have the funding or will to design new chipsets. Compaq was
> > not willing to spend the money.
>
>         Huh?? That's another cheap shot. Compaq had its own Alpha products
>         and chipsets. And       Samsung provided most of the funding!
>         As I said above, there's more to THIS story than you
>         know. This is not the forum for talking about it.
So, tell me the model of the new uni or dual processor Alpha chipset and 
board from Compaq?
Compaq spent a pile of money on Wildfire.
What else did they finish?
We kept hearing the rumours about the new chipsets, which never saw the 
light of day.

> > Now Samsung is using PC chipsets to deal with this, with some good
> > results. At least they ARE still doing something and making
> >progress, slowly but surely.
>
>         Yet another cheap shot. Using PC chipsets? What's on the
>         UP1000/UP1100?  A PC chipset!
Right, and it had horrible memory bandwidth and maximum RAM of 768MB.
Now they are using more modern DDR capable chipsets that have good 
bandwidth and 4GB capability. This is pure Samsung work, BTW, and API had 
little to do with it.


>         The cost of the AMD chipsets wasn't an issue. This issues
>         were:
>         1. The price of the CPU and the (EXPENSIVE!) cache
Yes, cache is expensive. Yes, small run boards are expensive.
More expensive, than say a PC164LX? Not by a factor of 3 or 4, which is 
what API charged for them.
It was a "chicken and egg" thing.
API wanted to make so much revenue on sales that the prices were at a level 
where the products did not "take off". So the volume stayed low, and so did 
the price.
In the meantime PC hardware kept advancing and getting cheaper.

>         2. The cost of making a board with more than 4 layers
>         3. Component prices based on a TINY build rate
>         4. The higher tolerances needed to support an Alpha platform
Agree with you on these points.

>         All these and more had a hell of alot more to do with
>         the costs of API or Samsung Alpha products than a chipset.

Yes, but outside of UP1x00 where do you see AGP support?
Where do you see DDR support?


>         Oh, did I mention that the low-end products didn't sell that
>         well? Why not? What does the UP1500 do better price wise
>         today that has you pushing it? Are you selling more than XXX
>         a month?

As I already said, maximum RAM size and speed was the issue.
The boards and CPUs worked fine, but the RAM bottleneck killed them.
Even an LX could do 1GB, and with better memory bandwidth. We heard all the 
rumours about API and Compaq doing new chipsets, but they never saw the 
light of day.

>         Could API and Samsung have done more/better? Of course, but
>         like any story, there are two sides; the public and
>         the private and the private should remain so.
Why? it is old history now.


>         There's alot of people out of work today that tried
>         really hard against great difficulty to turn out
>         products for you to sell. Did they make the best
>         decisions? It's easy to play Monday Morning Quarterback
>         and say they didn't. Those of us out of work today are
>         a little sensitive to your cheap shots that attempt to
>         point the blame in only one direction. Just let the
>         whole thing lie and move on, ok?
And all along the way they did not pay much attention to what their 
resellers were saying.
One by one they all dropped off the list.
Remember Carrera, Aspen, DCG,  and on and on..
Nobody I talked to at those companies had much to say that they were happy 
with API's decisions.

Understand this: A lot of good people worked at API.
Unfortunately it does not look like the management paid much attention to 
their opinions.

And as a dealer they were always tooting a horn about what exciting new 
deal they had signed with some new customer or another.
In the meantime, all the dealers who were actually selling Alphas, and had 
done so for a long time, were left in the lurch, short of parts, and 
getting treated like second hand citizens.
about 15 months ago they told us about the wonderful new UP2000+ boards and 
the 833 SlotB CPUs with 4MB of DDR cache.
We ordered, and were told we would get them in December. That actually took 
until June, and in the meantime they were busy suppling to a customer on a 
direct deal, bypassing their dealers, and using all their production capacity.

Seen the Cray CS20 clusters yet?

Anyway, I don't think the list need to hear more of this, so make your 
reply, and I will leave it at that.


With our best regards,

