On Tue, 15 Jan 2002, Maurice Hilarius wrote:

> For most of a year, after Samsung announced the UP1500, we would ask API if 
> they were going to be distributing it, and they said "maybe".

Speaking from the testing aspect, we had VERY few UP1500s to test and even
fewer details on the boards themselves to add the bits needed to test them
(kernel, etc).  When they finally came in in greater numbers (read: more
than 2), I know that I (personally) didn't have the time needed to even
boot one of the boards, not to mention actually put some coding effort
into them.  It's no small wonder, from my point of view, some of the
reasons that may have contributed to API's decision to not hurry when
saying something like: "let's put our names on this and ship it".

A few people took the time to boot some of the boards that we had, but I
don't think anyone that did had the knowledge to put the effort into
making the thing look like something other than a nautilus to the
kernel.  Lack of developer docs on the chipset didn't help much either (in
all fairness, I suspect it was because Samsung didn't have the docs
together at the time and drafts and boards were in short supply).

> In the meantime Samsung people made arrangements so that we could book 
> them, but not in North America.
> Finally API folded it's tent, and those silly days are over.

I'm going to withhold comment on this one...as Mike stated, there's more
to all of these stories than can be told outside of API's
(now former, I suppose) walls.

> Cheap shot? No I don't think so. I think API did as much as possible to 
> discourage Alpha development as they could. Perhaps not intentionally, but 
> that was the net effect.

What could've been done to encourage it?  I'm curious.

> So, tell me the model of the new uni or dual processor Alpha chipset and 
> board from Compaq?
> Compaq spent a pile of money on Wildfire.
> What else did they finish?
> We kept hearing the rumours about the new chipsets, which never saw the 
> light of day.

I'm not really privvy to most of what went on in Compaq, but it's no
surprise that many of the things in development never saw the light of
day, especially given economic conditions both within and outside of the
company.  Remember that downsizing became a fad again right at the
critical (wrong) time for many of the projects that I was aware of...

> Right, and it had horrible memory bandwidth and maximum RAM of 768MB.
> Now they are using more modern DDR capable chipsets that have good 
> bandwidth and 4GB capability. This is pure Samsung work, BTW, and API had 
> little to do with it.

OK, so what's on the UP1500?  A PC chipset!  Well, at least it was
designed as such, although not used as such for very long.

> Yes, cache is expensive. Yes, small run boards are expensive.

Don't forget the cost of manufacturing the motherboards themselves, which,
as Mike said, are more than 4 layers.  From someone "in the know" about
the cost of manufacturing those boards, I can assure you that it's
staggering compared to PC motherboards and much harder to find someone
willing to actually produce them to begin with.  I won't go into volume,
since many of the places that are capable of manufacturing such boards
either aren't set up for larger runs or ask for prohibitive amounts of
cash to do it.

> More expensive, than say a PC164LX? Not by a factor of 3 or 4, which is 
> what API charged for them.

Actually, yes, it can (and frequently does) cost more now to produce a
post-LX motherboard than it did to produce an LX in its day.  The boards
are more complex and, especially in recent years, the most common parts
are harder to find and thus more expensive, even in bulk (resistors,
capacitors, etc).  

> API wanted to make so much revenue on sales that the prices were at a level 
> where the products did not "take off". So the volume stayed low, and so did 
> the price.

I don't know about the above.  From what I gather, the margin on most of
the hardware wasn't as great as you assume above.

> In the meantime PC hardware kept advancing and getting cheaper.

Gee, I wonder why....could it be that PC hardware is simpler and easier to
manufacture?  Nah, that couldn't be it at all... :-)

> Yes, but outside of UP1x00 where do you see AGP support?
> Where do you see DDR support?

DDR support was just being introduced into chipsets around the time that I
was laid off by API, so the DDR argument is pretty much moot.  In fact,
there were no PC DDR motherboards publicly available at that time (DDR
cache support was available at that time pretty widely, though).  As far
as AGP support, I believed (and still do) that AGP support is not needed
in a server.  In fact, I'm even a bit inclined to believe that video cards
aren't even needed in server-class machines.  Workstations are another
story, but then again, very few workstation products have come out of API,
Samsung, or Compaq (on the Alpha side) and mot of those are dated anyway.

> >         Could API and Samsung have done more/better? Of course, but
> >         like any story, there are two sides; the public and
> >         the private and the private should remain so.
> Why? it is old history now.

Some things are better left dead and buried.  Also, some confidentiality
agreements may still be in effect, despite the demise of the company.

> And all along the way they did not pay much attention to what their 
> resellers were saying.
> One by one they all dropped off the list.
> Remember Carrera, Aspen, DCG,  and on and on..
> Nobody I talked to at those companies had much to say that they were happy 
> with API's decisions.

Resellers can suggest products, but my personal experience is that they
rarely drive design decisions.  Take that with as many grains of salt as
are needed, but more often than not, even the users' suggestions aren't
paid much attention to.  It's sometimes unfortunate, but I've seen the
other extreme as well (where everyone is listened to way too much) and
it's far worse on that extreme for the future of any company.

> Understand this: A lot of good people worked at API.
> Unfortunately it does not look like the management paid much attention to 
> their opinions.

I think it was a good place to work, albeit frustrating at times, but the
corporate dynamics (mostly with Samsung) were interesting to say the
least, and quite unique.  I'm not saying that it was "bad" or "good", just
unusual.

> And as a dealer they were always tooting a horn about what exciting new 
> deal they had signed with some new customer or another.

This is actually incredibly common in today's business world, and it's
unfortunate.  I'm personally very tired of a product being classified as
"released" just because the manufacturer made up an incredibly vague press
release about it rather than calling it "released" when the product
actually ships (which is usually six months to a year after the initial
announcement).  Also, "strategic relations" with other corporations or
major customers are used as a yardstick for investors now more than ever,
so to expect any of that to change anytime soon would be foolish.

> In the meantime, all the dealers who were actually selling Alphas, and had 
> done so for a long time, were left in the lurch, short of parts, and 
> getting treated like second hand citizens.

I think part of your frustration was something that I never understood
about API even before I worked there: why sell directly AND through
resellers?  I never heard (nor asked for) a reason while working there, so
I can't comment on reasoning from an API perspective (I was only a techie,
after all), but it never made too much sense to me. (read: personal
feelings, not observations from API days)

> Seen the Cray CS20 clusters yet?

Ugh...this I will comment on, and definitely not from an API
position: Cray's best engineers have been gone for quite some
time.  Those few that are left appear to be mired in the bureaucracy that
Cray apparently has created in the meantime.  Honestly, Cray's existing
customer base was hardly looking forward to many changes, so I'm sure that
there were internal battles going on about how "wanted" the new clusters
would be (which leads to foot-dragging).  What I can say is that my
dealings with Cray techs back in the 80s was far more engaging and
worthwhile than any of the contact that I had with them since 1995 or
so.  Now it seems that I end up spending more time explaining things to
them about their products than they spend talking to me.  So, it's not
much of a surprise that not much has left their doors since their last
product...

> Anyway, I don't think the list need to hear more of this, so make your 
> reply, and I will leave it at that.

Does this apply to me? ;-)

C




