I agree, it doesn't make sense.  When I first turn on the machine, I see a 
black screen with AlphaBIOS 5.68-2 on the top, with a window displaying a 
memory test (very Intel PC-esque).  When the memory test is done, the BIOS 
ids the SCSI card and such, then switch to a almost complete black screen 
that says:

	AlphaBIOS Version 5.68-2
	Please Select the Operating System to start:

	Linux RedHat 7.1 (highlighted)

	Press Enter to Continue.

If I press Enter, it then switches to the blue SRM screen, and the top line 
says 'OS_type: UNIX - console CIPCA driver not started'.  It then proceeds to 
boot, not giving me a prompt.  At one point, I see '/boot/vmlinuz-2.4.17' 
which gives me hope that it is booting my new kernel.  But, when it finishes, 
the login prompt tells me:
	Red Hat Linux release 7.1 (Seawolf)
	Kernel 2.4.12 on an alpha
Huh?

So I did a 'diff /boot/vmlinuz-2.4.17 
/usr/src/linux-2.4.17/arch/alpha/boot/vmlinux.gz' and got nothing.
I did a 'diff /boot/vmlinuz-2.4.17 
/usr/src/linux-2.4.12/arch/alpha/boot/vmlinx.gz' and got 'Binary files blah 
and blah differ', as I know already.

