Hello

What do you think about this: at home I have a Windows
XP machine used by us all (dont blame me :)). I also
have an Alpha Personal Workstation 500a (Miata), only
to be touched by me :). And then we have an old
Pentium 133 box.

Since the Windows XP machine needs to be used by
everyone and we have only one internet connection
available, I decided to use Internet Connection
Sharing to get the other 2 machines online too. As for
the P133 machine it works fine: the ethernet card is
configured for DHCP, it gets all parameters straight
and I can use samba, telnet into it, ping the XP
machine and the Alpha, and use every other service
using the hostnames, which are resolved by the XP
machine.

My Miata box has 2 network interfaces. I disabled the
first since it is not used yet, and enabled the other
one for DHCP at console level. I configured this box
identically to the P133 machine. I can reach its Samba
shares using the hostname, telnet into it using the
hostname, but... I cannot reach the P133 box by its
hostname! This is a pain since the IP is dynamically
and furthermore I do not see why it shouldn't work.

Basically the hostname of the Alpha gets resolved fine
on the P133 machine, but on the other hand the
hostname for the P133 box is not known at the Alpha
box. I discovered this when I was configuring NFS.

The Internet connection sharing should take care of
DHCP, DNS and all that. honestly not being Micro4oft
fan I must admit it worked fine so far.

One thing: the P133 is running httpd while the Alpha
is not. Could this make a difference and if it does,
why?

Hope this story makes some sence to anybody :)

Kind regards
Sam

