> to change the cursor shape, and sometimes this causes problems.
>
> Two other suggestions:
>
> 1. use a much later kernel, say 2.4.16/17, than the stock one in RH7.1
> 2. download and build XFree86 4.2.0 which is just released, I believe.
>
> 4.2.0 has a lot more fixes in the VX1 code, including multi-head,
> which has also been fixed for the ELSA Permedia 2's and Matrox
> Millennium I's. both the latter two have the VGA-disable DIP switches,
> which are not needed on VX1 (SRM console does some funky business in
> SW).
>
> I'm currently running a dual VX1 setup in a DS20, 4.2.0/xinerama/gnome,
> with reasonable stability.
>
> Of course, YMMV, but if it does, PLEASE LET ME KNOW!

Okay.  I'm letting you know, and letting the list hear a "thank
you".

Jay, thank you very, very much for recommending 4.2.0!!!

The new Redhat XFree86 4.1.0 did not solve the problems I was
seeing, but 4.2.0 compiled from source from xfree86.org seems to
have done the job.  I'm now using 24-bit color (which I had not
been able to do since I moved from RH6.1 to RH7.0).  The VX1 is
working very nicely.

In case it might be of some use to others with similar issues, I
put XFree86 4.2.0 in /usr/local/X11R6, made symlinks for the
app-defaults files not present in 4.2.0, added
/usr/local/X11R6/lib to /etc/ld.so.conf, and added
/usr/local/X11R6/bin to my path.  I also removed the suid bit
from the xterm binary.  (That one was scary!)

I have not yet updated the kernel to 2.4.17, but I think I will
give that a try in a few days.  I tried the SWCursor option, but
it did not seem to make a difference.  I'm getting quick-flash
gunk on the screen when moving the cursor around, when Netscape
is playing an animated ad, or when xawtv is running, either
little flecks or larger blobs, but they go away after a frame or
two.

For the other users of the system (the rest of the family), the
Backspace vs. Delete issue needs to be handled in their .cshrc
files, and I need to find the place to stop dropping sync to the
monitor after just a couple of minutes of inactivity.

Thanks.

Robert Riches
richesr1@inetarena.com




